<Article><Journal><ISOAbbreviation>J Dermatolog Treat</ISOAbbreviation><Title>Journal of dermatological treatment</Title></Journal>
<ArticleTitle>Real-world clinical experience with ruxolitinib cream versus systemic therapies in patients with moderate atopic dermatitis in the United States.</ArticleTitle>
<Abstract>
<AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Ruxolitinib cream is a topical JAK inhibitor approved for atopic dermatitis (AD).</AbstractText>
<AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data from the Adelphi AD Disease Specific Programme (n=619) were analysed.</AbstractText>
<AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Mean affected BSA decreased by 49% with ruxolitinib cream, 35% with systemic immunosuppressants, 61% with oral JAK inhibitors and 55% with biologics. Physician-reported severity improved in 48.4% of ruxolitinib cream patients. Results were similar in patients with BSA of 20% or less.</AbstractText>
<AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Ruxolitinib cream showed outcomes comparable to systemic therapies in moderate AD.</AbstractText>
</Abstract>
